    {
      "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56eo",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "56EO Privacy safeguard 12—security of CDR data, and destruction",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019#sch-1-sec-56eo"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019.pdf, p.64",
      "values:originalText": "or de-identification of redundant CDR data\n\n(1) Each person (a CDR entity) who is:\n\n(a) an accredited data recipient of CDR data; or\n\n(b) a designated gateway for CDR data;\n\nConsumer data right  Schedule 1\nMain amendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 63, 2019\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Consumer Data Right) Act 2019\n\nmust take the steps specified in the consumer data rules to protect\nthe CDR data from:\n\n(c) misuse, interference and loss; and\n\n(d) unauthorised access, modification or disclosure.\nNote:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\n\n(2) If:\n\n(a) the CDR entity no longer needs any of that CDR data for\neither of the following purposes (the redundant data):\n\n(i) a purpose permitted under the consumer data rules;\n\n(ii) a purpose for which the person is able to use or disclose\nit in accordance with this Division; and\n\n(b) the CDR entity is not required to retain the redundant data by\nor under an Australian law or a court/tribunal order; and\n\n(c) the redundant data does not relate to any current or\nanticipated:\n\n(i) legal proceedings; or\n\n(ii) dispute resolution proceedings;\n\nto which the CDR entity is a party;\nthe CDR entity must take the steps specified in the consumer data\nrules to destroy the redundant data or to ensure that the redundant\ndata is de-identified.\nNote 1:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\nNote 2:\nAustralian Privacy Principle 11 will not apply for paragraph (b) (see\nparagraph 56EC(4)(a) or (d)).\nSubdivision F—Correction of CDR data",
      "skos:definition": "or de-identification of redundant CDR data\n\n(1) Each person (a CDR entity) who is:\n\n(a) an accredited data recipient of CDR data; or\n\n(b) a designated gateway for CDR data;\n\nConsumer data right  Schedule 1\nMain amendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 63, 2019\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Consumer Data Right) Act 2019\n\nmust take the steps specified in the consumer data rules to protect\nthe CDR data from:\n\n(c) misuse, interference and loss; and\n\n(d) unauthorised access, modification or disclosure.\nNote:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\n\n(2) If:\n\n(a) the CDR entity no longer needs any of that CDR data for\neither of the following purposes (the redundant data):\n\n(i) a purpose permitted under the consumer data rules;\n\n(ii) a purpose for which the person is able to use or disclose\nit in accordance with this Division; and\n\n(b) the CDR entity is not required to retain the redundant data by\nor under an Australian law or a court/tribunal order; and\n\n(c) the redundant data does not relate to any current or\nanticipated:\n\n(i) legal proceedings; or\n\n(ii) dispute resolution proceedings;\n\nto which the CDR entity is a party;\nthe CDR entity must take the steps specified in the consumer data\nrules to destroy the redundant data or to ensure that the redundant\ndata is de-identified.\nNote 1:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\nNote 2:\nAustralian Privacy Principle 11 will not apply for paragraph (b) (see\nparagraph 56EC(4)(a) or (d)).\nSubdivision F—Correction of CDR data",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56eo-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56eo-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "28b144543f070068c9f57dea356d8df7aa38daf6b056a2732506024e00ee6537"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019#sch-1-sec-56eo",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "56EO Privacy safeguard 12—security of CDR data, and destruction",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 64,
      "values:originalText": "or de-identification of redundant CDR data\n\n(1) Each person (a CDR entity) who is:\n\n(a) an accredited data recipient of CDR data; or\n\n(b) a designated gateway for CDR data;\n\nConsumer data right  Schedule 1\nMain amendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 63, 2019\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Consumer Data Right) Act 2019\n\nmust take the steps specified in the consumer data rules to protect\nthe CDR data from:\n\n(c) misuse, interference and loss; and\n\n(d) unauthorised access, modification or disclosure.\nNote:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\n\n(2) If:\n\n(a) the CDR entity no longer needs any of that CDR data for\neither of the following purposes (the redundant data):\n\n(i) a purpose permitted under the consumer data rules;\n\n(ii) a purpose for which the person is able to use or disclose\nit in accordance with this Division; and\n\n(b) the CDR entity is not required to retain the redundant data by\nor under an Australian law or a court/tribunal order; and\n\n(c) the redundant data does not relate to any current or\nanticipated:\n\n(i) legal proceedings; or\n\n(ii) dispute resolution proceedings;\n\nto which the CDR entity is a party;\nthe CDR entity must take the steps specified in the consumer data\nrules to destroy the redundant data or to ensure that the redundant\ndata is de-identified.\nNote 1:\nThis subsection is a civil penalty provision (see section 56EU).\nNote 2:\nAustralian Privacy Principle 11 will not apply for paragraph (b) (see\nparagraph 56EC(4)(a) or (d)).\nSubdivision F—Correction of CDR data"
    },
