    {
      "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56gc",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "56GC Complying with requirements to provide CDR data:",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019#sch-1-sec-56gc"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019.pdf, p.88",
      "values:originalText": "protection from liability\n\n(1) If:\n\n(a) a CDR participant, or designated gateway, for CDR data (the\nCDR entity):\n\n(i) provides the CDR data to another person; or\n\nConsumer data right  Schedule 1\nMain amendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 63, 2019\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Consumer Data Right) Act 2019\n\n(ii) otherwise allows another person access to the CDR\ndata; and\n\n(b) the CDR entity does so, in good faith, in compliance with:\n\n(i) this Part; and\n\n(ii) regulations made for the purposes of this Part; and\n\n(iii) the consumer data rules;\nthe CDR entity is not liable to an action or other proceeding,\nwhether civil or criminal, for or in relation to the matter in\nparagraph (a).\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (1) for a criminal action or criminal proceeding (see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(2) A person who wishes to rely on subsection (1) in relation to a civil\naction or civil proceeding bears an evidential burden in relation to\nthat matter.\n\n(3) In this section:\nevidential burden, in relation to a matter, means the burden of\nadducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable\npossibility that the matter exists or does not exist.",
      "skos:definition": "protection from liability\n\n(1) If:\n\n(a) a CDR participant, or designated gateway, for CDR data (the\nCDR entity):\n\n(i) provides the CDR data to another person; or\n\nConsumer data right  Schedule 1\nMain amendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 63, 2019\nTreasury Laws Amendment (Consumer Data Right) Act 2019\n\n(ii) otherwise allows another person access to the CDR\ndata; and\n\n(b) the CDR entity does so, in good faith, in compliance with:\n\n(i) this Part; and\n\n(ii) regulations made for the purposes of this Part; and\n\n(iii) the consumer data rules;\nthe CDR entity is not liable to an action or other proceeding,\nwhether civil or criminal, for or in relation to the matter in\nparagraph (a).\nNote:\nA defendant bears an evidential burden in relation to the matter in\nsubsection (1) for a criminal action or criminal proceeding (see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code).\n\n(2) A person who wishes to rely on subsection (1) in relation to a civil\naction or civil proceeding bears an evidential burden in relation to\nthat matter.\n\n(3) In this section:\nevidential burden, in relation to a matter, means the burden of\nadducing or pointing to evidence that suggests a reasonable\npossibility that the matter exists or does not exist.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56gc-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:treasury-laws-amendment-consumer-data-right-act-2019-sch-1-sec-56gc-ss-2"
        },
